There's logic deeply embedded in the random-number generation code
that
causes it to select numbers that will result in harm to your
character.

The intelligent monsters with wands of death when you have neither
reflection nor MR are only one of many manifestations of this.  Among
other things, if you stand near the castle drawbridge, one of the
monsters behind it *will* have a wand of striking.  The probability of
stumbling into a polymorph trap increased dramatically if you are
wearing the only non-cursed mithril in the dungeon, *especially* if
your
character's strength is too low to bear the weight of ring mail.
Monsters that always drop edible, high-nutrition corpses are never
generated when you desperately need food, unless you are already too
weak from hunger to fight them.  Eating tengu corpses almost always
gives you teleportitis first, before teleport control.  Leaving your
tinning kit in a stash because you aren't strong enough to bear the
weight virtually guarantees you will encounter at least two giant
zombies before you get back to that stash.  When a monster "randomly"
curses just one of your possessions, at least two thirds of the time
it's the bag of holding -- unless you are wearing a shield and either
that or your primary weapon is already cursed, in which case the curse
will always land on the thing that causes you to have no free hands.
The list goes on and on and on.

Basically, if the so-called "random" number generator can, by
judicious
selection of just the right output, harm your character in some
material
way, it does so.  Sometimes it waits a while, to give you the illusion
that there was some preventative measure you could have taken, but it
always gets you in the end.

